        "en": "He said: Abu Isa Ubaydullah ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad ibn Hilal al-Ta'i al-Basri (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to me, Abu Uthman Sa'id ibn Muhammad narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Sallam ibn Yasar [Sayyar] al-Kufi narrated to me, Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to me, Isa ibn Abi Shaybah al-Qadi narrated to me, Nu'h ibn Darraj narrated to me, Qudamah ibn Zaydah narrated to me from his father, who said: Ali ibn al-Husayn (peace be upon him) said: 'It reached me, O Zaydah, that you sometimes visit the grave of Abu Abdillah al-Husayn (peace be upon him).' I said: 'Indeed, that is as it reached you.' He said to me: 'Why do you do that, while you have a place with your Sultan who does not compel anyone to love us, prefer us, and mention our virtues, and the due of our right upon this Ummah?' I said: 'By Allah, I do not intend by that except Allah and His Messenger, and I do not mind the anger of the angry, nor does anything unpleasant that befalls me because of it seem great in my chest.' He said: 'By Allah, indeed that is so.' I said: 'By Allah, indeed that is so.' He said it three times, and I said it three times. He said: 'Rejoice, then rejoice, then rejoice, for I will surely inform you of news that was with me in the treasured reserve [sea]. Indeed, when what befell us at al-Tabb occurred, and my father (peace be upon him) was killed, and those who were with him from his children, brothers, and all his family were killed, and his womenfolk were carried on saddles, and Kufa was intended for us, I kept looking at them fallen and not buried. That became great in my chest, and my anxiety over what I saw of them intensified, and my soul was about to depart. My aunt Zaynab al-Kubra bint Ali (peace be upon her) noticed that in me and said: 'What is it that I see you giving up your soul, O remainder of my grandfather, father, and brothers?' I said: 'How can I not grieve and lament when I see my master, my brothers, my uncles, my father's children, and my family lying dead in their blood, covered in dust on the open ground, stripped, not shrouded, not buried, and no one passes by them nor approaches them, as if they were a household from the Daylam and Khazar?' She said: 'Let not what you see grieve you. By Allah, indeed that is a covenant from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) to your grandfather, your father, and your uncle. And indeed Allah took the covenant [of a group of this Ummah whom the leaders of this Ummah do not know, but they are known in the inhabitants of the heavens] that they will gather these scattered limbs and bury them, and these bruised bodies, and set up a sign for this event for the grave of your master, the Master of the Martyrs, whose trace will not fade and whose mark will not disappear through the succession of nights and days. And the leaders of disbelief and the followers of error will strive to erase it and flatten it, but its trace will not increase except in prominence, and its matter will not increase except in elevation.' I said: 'And what is this covenant and what is this news?' She said: 'Yes, Umm Ayman told me that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) visited the house of Fatimah (peace be upon her) on one of the days, and she prepared for him a harirah (a type of food). And Ali (peace be upon him) came to him with a plate containing dates. Then Umm Ayman said: So I brought them a gourd containing milk and butter. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), Ali, Fatimah, al-Hasan, and al-Husayn (peace be upon them) ate from that harirah. And the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) drank, and they drank from that milk. Then he ate, and they ate from that date and butter. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) washed his hand, while Ali was pouring water over it. When he finished washing his hand, he wiped his face. Then he looked at Ali, Fatimah, al-Hasan, and al-Husayn with a look by which we recognized joy in his face. Then he cast his gaze toward the sky for a long time. Then he turned his face toward the Qiblah, spread his hands, and supplicated. Then he fell prostrating, sobbing. He prolonged his sobbing, his wailing rose, and his tears flowed. Then he raised his head and bowed to the ground, and his tears were dripping as if it were the rain's downpour. Fatimah, Ali, al-Hasan, and al-Husayn (peace be upon them) were saddened, and I was saddened with them because of what we saw of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). We hoped that he would ask him until, when that prolonged, Ali said to him, and Fatimah said to him: 'What is making you cry, O Messenger of Allah? May Allah not dry your eyes? What we see of your state has ulcerated our hearts.' He said: 'O my brother, I am pleased with you.' And Muzahim ibn al-Warith said in his hadith here: 'O my beloved, indeed I am pleased with you with a pleasure I have never been pleased with like it. And indeed I look at you and praise Allah for His favor upon me in you, when Jibril (peace be upon him) descended to me and said: O Muhammad, indeed Allah, Blessed and Exalted, looked at what is in your heart and knew your pleasure with your brother, your daughter, and your two grandsons, so He perfected the favor for you and congratulated you with the gift by making them and their descendants, their lovers, and their followers with you in Paradise. He does not separate you from them; they love as you love, and are given as you are given, until you are pleased, and above pleasure, over much trial that befalls them in the world and hardships that strike them by the hands of people who claim your religion and claim that they are from your Ummah, being excused from Allah and from you, randomly, randomly, and killing, killing. Their places of fall are distant, their graves are a choice from Allah for them and for you. So praise Allah, Exalted and Majestic, for His choice, and be pleased with His decree.' So I praised Allah and was pleased with His decree for what He chose for you. Then Jibril said to me: 'O Muhammad, indeed your brother will be oppressed after you, conquered over your Ummah, afflicted by your enemies, then killed after you. His killer will be the worst of creation and the most wretched of beings. He will be like the barren camel in a land to which his Hijrah will be, and it will be the nursery of his followers and the followers of his children. And in it, in every state, their trials will increase and their calamity will grow great. And indeed this grandson of yours,' and he pointed with his hand to al-Husayn (peace be upon him), 'will be killed in a group from your descendants and your family and the best of your Ummah on the bank of the Euphrates in a land called Karbala. Because of it, hardship and trial will increase upon your enemies and the enemies of your descendants on the day whose hardship will not end and whose regret will not perish. And it is the sweetest valley of the earth and the greatest in honor. Your grandson and his family will be killed in it, and it is from the flat expanse of Paradise. When that day comes when your grandson and his family are killed, and the troops of the people of disbelief and curse surround him, the earth will tremble from its corners, the mountains will surge, and their agitation will increase, and the seas will clash with their waves, and the heavens will roar with their inhabitants, angry for you, O Muhammad, and for your descendants, and in appreciation of what violates your honor and the evil with which you will be repaid in your descendants and progeny. And nothing of that will remain except that Allah, Exalted and Majestic, will ask permission in the victory of your family—the oppressed, the wronged, who are Allah's proof upon His creation after you. So Allah will reveal to the heavens and the earth, and the mountains and the seas, and those in them: "
